<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/drop_buffer_pool.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="sql-dropbufferpool">
 <indexterm zone="sql-dropbufferpool">
  <primary>DROP BUFFER POOL</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>DROP BUFFER POOL</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>SQL - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>DROP BUFFER POOL</refname>
  <refpurpose>remove a buffer pool</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
DROP BUFFER POOL [ IF EXISTS ] <replaceable class="parameter">name</replaceable> [, ...] [ CASCADE | RESTRICT ]
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>DROP BUFFER POOL</command> removes a buffer pool from the system.
   The pool's dynamic shared memory and its background trickle writer are torn
   down; the pool's cached pages are discarded, but the data those pages cache
   is unaffected on disk.
  </para>

  <para>
   Only superusers can drop a buffer pool.  The always-present
   <literal>default</literal> pool cannot be dropped, and a pool cannot be
   dropped during recovery.
  </para>

  <para>
   A pool cannot be dropped while any relation still refers to it through its
   <literal>buffer_pool</literal> storage parameter; remove or change that
   storage parameter on the dependent relations first.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>IF EXISTS</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Do not throw an error if the buffer pool does not exist. A notice is
      issued in this case.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The name of a buffer pool.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>CASCADE</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      This option currently has no additional effect: a relation's use of a
      pool through the <literal>buffer_pool</literal> storage parameter is not
      an automatically droppable dependency, so the <command>DROP</command>
      still fails while such a relation exists.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>RESTRICT</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Refuse to drop the buffer pool if any object depends on it.  This is the
      default.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   To remove buffer pool <literal>analytics</literal>:
<programlisting>
DROP BUFFER POOL analytics;
</programlisting></para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   <command>DROP BUFFER POOL</command> is a
   <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> extension.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>See Also</title>

  <simplelist type="inline">
   <member><xref linkend="sql-createbufferpool"/></member>
   <member><xref linkend="sql-alterbufferpool"/></member>
  </simplelist>
 </refsect1>

</refentry>
